    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d882">

                <pb facs="1178" n="1164" xml:id="pg1164"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">493.11/10–1844: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Appointed Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Hurley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-12-12T15:00:00-04:00">December 12,
                            1944—3 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1626. Unless you perceive objection to so doing, please see the Foreign Minister
                    and inquire as to the present status of the proposal for the dissolution of the
                    China Foundation (ReEmbs 1704, October 18, 4 p.m.). For your information and for
                    possible use in your conversation with Soong, the mechanics and legal
                    complications involved in such dissolution by December 31, in so far as the
                    Foundation’s obligations and funds in the United States are concerned, present
                    an almost insurmountable task. You will understand and cause Soong to understand
                    in the event that you decide to mention this phase of the matter, that the
                    foregoing is separate and apart from our fundamental concern over the proposed
                    dissolution of the Foundation.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
